e that ω(G) ≤3 in the\ngraph G = (V, E).\nIf the edge set can be partitioned into two sets\nE′ ∪E′′ = E in such a way that each induced P3 of G has precisely one edge\nin each of E′ and E′′, then χℓ(G) = χ(G) = ω(G).\nFurther problems.\nGraphs with larger chromatic number are considered\nin recent works by Gravier and Maﬀray. In [76] they investigate graphs in\nwhich there exists a k-coloring without color classes of more than 2 vertices.\nCorollaries are derived for claw-free graphs (i.e., graphs containing no in-\nduced star of degree 3) of small order, from which it follows that if G is\nthe complement of a triangle-free graph, then χ(G) = χℓ(G). An interesting\nrelated problem is\nConjecture 3.6. (Gravier, Maﬀray [75, 76])\nIf\nG is claw-free, then\nχℓ(G) = χ(G).\nIn some sense, this conjecture seems to be ‘ too strong,’ and perhaps it\nwould be worth making further eﬀorts to ﬁnd a counterexample. On the\nother hand, if it turns out to be true, then it implies the validity of the--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nH:/Thesis/Masters/Masters.dvi (https://escholarship.mcgill.ca/downloads/wp988p22v?locale=en)\nciteturn3search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.5 years ago; It is easily observed that the line graph of a multigraph is claw-free,⁶ and therefore Gravier and Maffray’s Conjecture would imply the List Colouring Conjecture.\n2.3 Chromatic-Choosable Graphs\n\nIn general, we say that a graph which satisfies ch = χ is chromatic-choosable [Ohb02].\n\nApart from the List Colouring Conjecture, there are several intriguing conjectures which claim that graphs of special classes are chromatic-choosable. One such conjecture was proposed by Gravier and Maffray in [GM97].\n\n**Gravier and Maffray’s Conjecture** ([GM97]). *Every claw-free graph is chromatic-choosable.*\n\nIt is easily observed that the line graph of a multigraph is claw-free,⁶ and therefore Gravier and Maffray’s Conjecture would imply the List Colouring Conjecture.\n\nIn [GM97], Gravier and Maffray verified their conjecture for a special class of claw-free graphs known as 3-colourable *elementary* graphs.⁷\n\nIn [BKW97], Borodin, Kostochka and Woodall proposed a version of the List Colouring Conjecture for total graphs.\n\n**The List Total Colouring Conjecture** ([BKW97]). *For every multigraph G, ch(T(G)) = χ(T(G)).*\n\nThis conjecture seems to fit well with other problems in the area. It is widely believed that the colouring properties of total graphs are quite similar to those of line graphs. This is perhaps best illustrated by the relation between a famous result of Vizing [Viz64] and the well studied Total Colouring Conjecture, made independently by Behzad [Beh65] and Vizing [Viz68]. For every graph G, Vizing’s Theorem says that Δ(G) ≤ χ(L(G)) ≤ Δ(G) + 1 and the Total Colouring Conjecture claims that\n\n⁶ This follows from the simple fact that every edge has only two endpoints.\n\n⁷ A graph is *elementary* if its edges can be coloured with two colours such that any induced path on three vertices contains edges of both colours.\n\n12--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStéphan is 50! (https://perso.isima.fr/~lucpasto/files/lucas-pastor-stephan-is-50.pdf)\nciteturn3search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.1 years ago; Green-outlined box containing the text: “Conjecture Gravier, Maffray 1997. Every claw-free graph G satisfies χₑ(G) = χ(G).”\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nGreen-outlined box containing the text: “Conjecture Vizing, Gupta, Albertson. Every line-graph G satisfies χₑ(G) = χ(G).”\n</visual_element>\n\nGeneralized into:\n\n<visual_element id=\"e2\">\nGreen-outlined box containing the text: “Conjecture Gravier, Maffray 1997. Every claw-free graph G satisfies χₑ(G) = χ(G).”\n</visual_element>\n\n2/17--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ngraphs \\(H\\) and \\(F\\) such that \\(\\chi(H)\\ge \\chi(F)\\), but we are not aware of any results besides those for \\(\\mathrm{ex}(n,C_{2r+1},C_{2k+1})\\) and consequences of Theorem 2.2. There are many individual open problems of this flavor. For example, the exact value of \\(\\mathrm{ex}(n,C_5,B_k)\\) is unknown (the asymptotics follow from Theorem 2.2 and the value of \\(\\mathrm{ex}(n,C_5,C_3)\\)). (https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/download/ds27/pdf/)\nciteturn3search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; *Any bipartite graph \\(H\\) with diameter at most 4 is \\(K_3\\)-Turán-good.\ngraphs \\(H\\) and \\(F\\) such that \\(\\chi(H)\\ge \\chi(F)\\), but we are not aware of any results besides those for \\(\\mathrm{ex}(n,C_{2r+1},C_{2k+1})\\) and consequences of Theorem 2.2. There are many individual open problems of this flavor. For example, the exact value of \\(\\mathrm{ex}(n,C_5,B_k)\\) is unknown (the asymptotics follow from Theorem 2.2 and the value of \\(\\mathrm{ex}(n,C_5,C_3)\\)).\n\nRecall that a graph \\(H\\) is \\(F\\)-Turán-good if, for \\(n\\) large enough, \\(\\mathrm{ex}(n,H,F)\\) is attained by the Turán graph with \\(\\chi(F)-1\\) partition classes and \\(H\\) is weakly \\(F\\)-Turán-good if, for \\(n\\) large enough, \\(\\mathrm{ex}(n,H,F)\\) is attained by an \\(n\\)-vertex complete \\((\\chi(F)-1)\\)-partite graph. The following concrete conjecture on Turán-goodness appears in [111] (see Subsection 3.2 for more details).\n\n**Conjecture 6.1** (Grzesik, Győri, Salia, and Tompkins [111]). *Any bipartite graph \\(H\\) with diameter at most 4 is \\(K_3\\)-Turán-good.*\n\nLet \\(k(H)\\) be the minimum threshold such that if \\(k \\ge k(H)\\), then \\(H\\) is \\(K_k\\)-Turán good. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTHÈSE\nPour obtenir le grade de\nDOCTEUR DE LA \nCOMM (https://perso.isima.fr/~lucpasto/files/thesis.pdf)\nciteturn3search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.2 years ago; Gravier and Maffray to generalize the List Coloring Conjecture to all claw-free graphs, ... A clique cutset in a graph G is a clique C of G such that G \\ C is discon-\n4.2 STRUCTURE OF CLAW-FREE PERFECT GRAPHS | 71\nX\nY\n1\nx1\n1\n2\n2\n1\ny1\nx2\n1\n2\nx1y1\nx2y1\nx2y2\nx3y2\n2\ny2\nx3\nx4y3\n2\nx3y3\nx4y2\n2\n3\n3\n3\n3\nx4\ny3\n1\nx4y3\n1\nL(B)\nB\nFigure 4.3: An example of Galvin’s theorem with a bipartite multigraph B and its\nline-graph L(B).\nGravier and Maffray to generalize the List Coloring Conjecture to all claw-free graphs,\nmore precisely, the following was conjectured in [37, 38].\nCONJECTURE 4.9\nEvery claw-free graph G satisﬁes ch(G) = χ(G).\nEven though this conjecture appears to be somehow too large, it is still widely\nopen. An interesting subclass of claw-free graphs concerning this problem is the claw-\nfree perfect graphs class.\n4.2 Structure of claw-free perfect graphs\nClaw-free perfect graphs are described by a decomposition theorem of Chvátal and\nSbihi [20]. A clique cutset in a graph G is a clique C of G such that G \\ C is discon-\nnected. A minimal clique cutset is a clique cutset that does not contain another clique\ncutset. Graph decomposition is a remarkable tool in many aspects of graph theory\nand allows to solve some of the most difﬁcult problems by reducing the difﬁculties\nto easy (with the regard to the problem one wants to solve) classes of graphs and\ncombining the solutions of multiple easy graphs to obtain a general solution for the\ninitial graph. Given a graph G, a decomposition of G is a pair (G1, G2) where G1\nand G2 are proper induced subgraphs of G. Many different types of decomposition--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStrong Target Conjecture — MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/368019/strong-target-conjecture)\nciteturn3search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Strong Target Conjecture ... source: Matheus Adauto, Viktoriya Bardenova, Yunus Bidav and Glenn Hurlbert, “Target Pebbling in Trees”, arXiv:2504.10460 (2026).\n\n# \n\nStrong Target Conjecture\n\nConjecture open\n\nLet $G$G be a graph, let $D$D be a target, let $|D|$∣D∣ denote its total demand, and let $s(D)$s(D) denote the parameter used in the source.\n\nStrong Target Conjecture. Every graph $G$G satisfies\n\n$$\\pi(G,D)\\leq \\pi_{|D|}(G)-s(D)+1$$π(G,D)≤π∣D∣​(G)−s(D)+1\n\nfor every target $D$D.\n\nThe source reports that this stronger inequality is known for trees and powers of paths, but remains unproved for general graphs.\n\n## Progress summary\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\nsource: Matheus Adauto, Viktoriya Bardenova, Yunus Bidav and Glenn Hurlbert, “Target Pebbling in Trees”, arXiv:2504.10460 (2026).\n\n## References\n\n2 papers in this index state this conjecture (2021–2025). The statement above is taken from the most recent of them; the others are arXiv:2112.09753.\n\ngraph-pebblinggraph-theory\n\n0\n\nposted by arXiv 2 days ago\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nElementary graphs are chromatic-choosable (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.16258)\nciteturn3academia18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; We prove that every elementary graph $G$ satisfies $\\ch(G)=χ(G)=ω(G)$, confirming for this class the conjecture of Gravier and Maffray that claw-free graphs are chromatic-choosable. The proof combines the Maffray--Reed description of elementary graphs as augmentations of line graphs of bipartite multigraphs, Galvin's theorem, an extremal (edge-maximal) choice of counterexample, and a Hall-type lemma describing how list colourings of a complete graph minus a matching can fail.\nTitle: Elementary graphs are chromatic-choosable\nAuthors: Narayanan N, Nandana K. Vasudevan, K. Somasundaram\nDate: Mon Oct 12 06:32:58 2026\n\nA graph is \\emph{elementary} if its edges can be coloured with two colours so that the two edges of every induced path on three vertices receive different colours. Elementary graphs are one of the two basic classes in the Chvátal--Sbihi decomposition of claw-free perfect graphs; they include all line graphs of bipartite multigraphs and all complements of bipartite graphs. We prove that every elementary graph $G$ satisfies $\\ch(G)=χ(G)=ω(G)$, confirming for this class the conjecture of Gravier and Maffray that claw-free graphs are chromatic-choosable. The proof combines the Maffray--Reed description of elementary graphs as augmentations of line graphs of bipartite multigraphs, Galvin's theorem, an extremal (edge-maximal) choice of counterexample, and a Hall-type lemma describing how list colourings of a complete graph minus a matching can fail.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the choosability of claw-free perfect graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.02576)\nciteturn3academia19 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.4 years ago; Authors: Sylvain Gravier, Frédéric Maffray, Lucas Pastor ... Based on this decomposition we prove that the conjecture holds true for every claw-free perfect graph with maximum clique size at most $4$.\nTitle: On the choosability of claw-free perfect graphs\nAuthors: Sylvain Gravier, Frédéric Maffray, Lucas Pastor\nDate: Mon Jun  8 16:32:05 2015\n\nIt has been conjectured that for every claw-free graph $G$ the choice number of $G$ is equal to its chromatic number. We focus on the special case of this conjecture where $G$ is perfect. Claw-free perfect graphs can be decomposed via clique-cutset into two special classes called elementary graphs and peculiar graphs. Based on this decomposition we prove that the conjecture holds true for every claw-free perfect graph with maximum clique size at most $4$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPebbling in powers of paths - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0012365X23000018)\nciteturn3search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.5 years ago; Crawled